        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Choose the type before the model.</strong> A mini or twist
            stepper is cheap, small and good for short bursts of extra movement; a handlebar model
            adds support; and only a full-size magnetic stepper or a stepmill gives the workout people
            remember from the gym. On the day we checked that meant about $50 to $200 for the first
            three types and from about $1,000 for the fourth.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then check the weight limit against more than one source, read the manual's time limit
            on any hydraulic stepper, measure your ceiling for a full-size machine, and run a recall
            check on anything bought second-hand. If you are still weighing a stepper against a
            treadmill, read
            <a href="/stairmaster-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">StairMaster vs treadmill</a>
            before you buy.
          </p>
